# BUG: list/fold hands a corrupted accumulator back in native code.
#
# This is the CommonMark port's block parser, cut down automatically to the
# declarations needed to show the fault (353 lines in cm/). It folds the
# input's lines through `parse_line` with `list/fold`, threading a parser
# state record that holds a list of frame records, then prints each open
# frame's `start` field.
#
#   kanso run bugs/fold_corrupts_state/main.kso --interp < input.md
#     1 1 5 6 7 7                       (correct)
#   kanso run bugs/fold_corrupts_state/main.kso < input.md
#     1 1 [list/broken_link []] 6 7 7   (wrong: an int field now holds a
#                                        std/list internal value)
#
# `kanso build --release` gives the same wrong answer. In the full port the
# same fault showed up as a segfault, as "`list/broken_link` has no field
# `kind`", as "`-` is not defined for these values" and as "no overload of
# `continues` matches these arguments", depending on the input.
#
# Replacing `list/fold lines start parse_line` in cm/blocks.kso with a
# hand-written recursion over the line index makes native agree with the
# interpreter, so the port does that. The same fault appeared with list/fold
# and a closure in the HTML renderer, worked around the same way.
import "./cm"
import "std/io"

io/stdin .> (s -> print (cm/starts s))
